        "message": "A lot of parental resistance to \"design\" comes from assuming it means unpredictable freelance gigs or unstable contract work. In reality, many design paths offer steady salaries, benefits, and clear career ladders. Here are three ways to bridge that gap practically: 1. **Highlight salaried design roles.** Look into in-house corporate design, UI/UX, product design, or digital marketing design. Pull up actual job listings in your area showing starting salaries, benefits, and required skills. Showing that you're aiming for structured, salaried positions directly addresses their concern about stability.\n2. **Set a timeline with measurable milestones.** Decide on a concrete window (for example, 6 to 12 months) to build a portfolio, complete training, or secure an entry-level role. Having a defined timeframe and a clear backup plan shows them you are treating your career seriously and practically, not just taking a gamble.\n3. **Consider a bridge approach.** If you need immediate financial stability or want to lower the stakes right now, take a reliable job that covers your living expenses while spending your evenings or weekends building your design portfolio and taking small projects. Once your design income or job offers are steady, you can make the full transition.",
